// FiqhHub — Salat (Prayer) Fatwas
window.FATWAS_SALAT = [
{id:'s001',cat:'salat',sub:'Obligatory Prayers',marja:'sistani',q:'What are the five daily obligatory prayers and their rak\'at?',a:'The five daily obligatory prayers are: (1) Fajr — 2 rak\'at, from dawn to sunrise. (2) Dhuhr — 4 rak\'at, from midday to sunset. (3) Asr — 4 rak\'at, from midday to sunset (after Dhuhr). (4) Maghrib — 3 rak\'at, after sunset until midnight. (5) Isha — 4 rak\'at, after sunset until midnight (after Maghrib). The total is 17 rak\'at daily. Each prayer has its specific time, though some can share time windows.',source:'Tawdih al-Masail, Ruling 729',tags:['daily prayers','times','rakat','obligatory']},
{id:'s002',cat:'salat',sub:'Obligatory Prayers',marja:'sistani',q:'What are the conditions for prayer to be valid?',a:'The conditions for valid prayer include: (1) Time — prayer must be within its prescribed time. (2) Qiblah — one must face the Ka\'bah. (3) Clothing — must be tahir, not usurped, not made from haram animal parts; for men, must cover from navel to knees; for women, must cover entire body except face and hands. (4) Place — must be tahir where sajdah is performed, must be stable, the place for sajdah must not be significantly higher or lower than feet. (5) Wudu/Ghusl/Tayammum. (6) Intention (niyyah). (7) The person must be of sound mind.',source:'Minhaj al-Salihin, Salat',tags:['prayer','conditions','validity','requirements']},
{id:'s003',cat:'salat',sub:'Qiblah',marja:'sistani',q:'How should one determine the qiblah direction?',a:'One should determine the qiblah direction through: (1) Personal certainty. (2) Testimony of two reliable (adil) persons. (3) A reliable compass or qiblah application that gives confident results. (4) If none of these provide certainty, one should pray in the direction they believe is most likely correct. If no probable direction can be determined, they should pray in four directions as a precaution. In prayer, a minor deviation from the exact qiblah direction is tolerable as long as one is generally facing the correct direction.',source:'Tawdih al-Masail, Ruling 778',tags:['qiblah','direction','compass','determination']},
{id:'s004',cat:'salat',sub:'Qiblah',marja:'khamenei',q:'What is the ruling on praying on an airplane?',a:'Prayer on an airplane is obligatory at its proper time and should not be delayed merely because one is on a plane. If the person can stand and face the qiblah, they must do so. If standing is not possible or causes significant hardship, they may pray sitting. If the plane changes direction during prayer, they should adjust to face the qiblah as much as possible. If determining the exact qiblah is difficult, they should pray in the direction they believe to be most likely correct. Ruku and sujud should be performed as normally as possible, even if only by nodding.',source:'Ajwibat al-Istifta\'at',tags:['airplane','travel','qiblah','sitting','prayer']},
{id:'s005',cat:'salat',sub:'Congregational',marja:'sistani',q:'What is the ruling on praying behind a Sunni imam in congregation?',a:'It is permissible to pray behind a Sunni imam for maintaining Muslim unity and in situations where a Shia congregation is not available (such as in Masjid al-Haram, Masjid al-Nabawi, or workplaces). The prayer should be performed with one\'s own Shia intention. One should recite al-Hamd and surah themselves silently. The prayer counts as congregation (jama\'ah) and is valid. This is from the principles of taqiyyah and preserving Muslim unity which the Imams (as) emphasized.',source:'al-Fiqh lil-Mughtaribin',tags:['congregation','sunni','unity','taqiyyah','prayer']},
{id:'s006',cat:'salat',sub:'Congregational',marja:'khamenei',q:'Can a woman lead congregational prayer?',a:'A woman can lead congregational prayer for other women only. She cannot lead men in congregational prayer. When leading women, the female imam should stand in the same row as the followers (not ahead of them). She can also lead mixed-gender congregational prayers where all followers are women and there are no male followers. The condition of a male imam is required when men are among the congregation.',source:'Ajwibat al-Istifta\'at',tags:['women','imam','congregation','leading','prayer']},
{id:'s007',cat:'salat',sub:'Traveler Prayer',marja:'sistani',q:'When must a traveler shorten their prayers (qasr)?',a:'A traveler must shorten 4-rak\'at prayers to 2 rak\'at when: (1) The journey distance is at least 8 farsakh (approximately 44 km / 27 miles) one way. (2) The person intends to travel this distance from the outset. (3) The traveler does not intend to stay in one place for 10 or more days during the journey. (4) The travel is not for sinful purposes. (5) The person is not a professional traveler (someone whose occupation involves constant travel). (6) The person has passed the point of tarakhkhus (where they can no longer hear the adhan of their city). Fajr and Maghrib prayers are not shortened.',source:'Tawdih al-Masail, Ruling 1271',tags:['travel','qasr','shortening','distance','prayer']},
{id:'s008',cat:'salat',sub:'Traveler Prayer',marja:'makarem',q:'What is the ruling for someone who regularly commutes between two cities for work?',a:'A person who travels regularly between two cities for work (such as daily or several times a week) is considered a frequent traveler (kathir al-safar). After a period of regularity (approximately 10 trips or the travel becoming their habitual pattern), they should pray full (tamam) prayers during their commute, not qasr. This applies to all regular commuters: teachers, students, businesspeople who regularly travel the same route. If they make a different, non-routine journey, they pray qasr for that trip.',source:'Tawdih al-Masail, Travel',tags:['commute','frequent traveler','work','qasr','tamam']},
{id:'s009',cat:'salat',sub:'Sajdah',marja:'sistani',q:'What surfaces are valid for sajdah (prostration)?',a:'Sajdah must be performed on earth or things that grow from earth (that are not eaten as food or worn as clothing). Valid surfaces include: soil, sand, stone, clay tablets (turbah), leaves and bark of trees, grass, straw. Invalid surfaces include: food items (wheat, rice, fruit), items of clothing (cotton, linen when in fabric form), synthetic materials, carpet, leather, metal. A turbah (clay tablet, especially from Karbala) is the recommended surface. Wood and paper are permissible for sajdah.',source:'Tawdih al-Masail, Ruling 1052',tags:['sajdah','surface','turbah','earth','prostration']},
{id:'s010',cat:'salat',sub:'Sajdah',marja:'shirazi',q:'Is it permissible to prostrate on paper or cardboard?',a:'Yes, prostrating on paper and cardboard is permissible because they are made from wood pulp, which originates from trees — things that grow from the earth and are not eaten as food or worn as clothing. Paper, whether plain or printed, is a valid surface for sajdah. This applies to newspapers, notebooks, cardboard, and similar products. It is a practical alternative when a turbah is not available.',source:'Tawdih al-Masail',tags:['sajdah','paper','cardboard','prostration']},
{id:'s011',cat:'salat',sub:'Friday Prayer',marja:'khamenei',q:'Is Friday prayer (Salat al-Jumu\'ah) obligatory?',a:'During the occultation of the Twelfth Imam (aj), Friday prayer is wajib takhyiri — meaning it is obligatory as a choice: one can pray either the Friday prayer (2 rak\'at with two khutbahs) or the regular Dhuhr prayer (4 rak\'at). If Friday prayer is established with proper conditions (a just imam leading it, minimum attendance), attending it is highly recommended and if one attends, it replaces the Dhuhr prayer. In the Islamic Republic, Friday prayer is established and strongly recommended.',source:'Ajwibat al-Istifta\'at',tags:['friday','jumuah','obligatory','dhuhr','occultation']},
{id:'s012',cat:'salat',sub:'Friday Prayer',marja:'sistani',q:'What is the ruling on Salat al-Jumu\'ah during the Greater Occultation?',a:'According to obligatory precaution, if Friday prayer is properly established with all its conditions in a locality, one should either attend it or pray Dhuhr. If one attends Friday prayer performed with all conditions, it suffices in place of Dhuhr prayer. The conditions include: a just imam, two khutbahs before the prayer addressing Islamic affairs, and minimum attendance. During the Greater Occultation, its obligatory nature is a matter of precaution rather than definitive obligation.',source:'Minhaj al-Salihin',tags:['friday','jumuah','occultation','precaution']},
{id:'s013',cat:'salat',sub:'Adhan & Iqamah',marja:'sistani',q:'Is adhan and iqamah obligatory before prayer?',a:'Adhan and iqamah before daily obligatory prayers are recommended (mustahab mu\'akkad) — they are not obligatory. However, they are so strongly recommended that abandoning them is highly disliked (makruh). For women, they are also recommended but the recommendation is less emphasized. In congregational prayer, if the adhan and iqamah have already been recited by the mu\'adhin, individual worshippers do not need to recite them again. They are not prescribed for non-daily prayers like Salat al-Ayat.',source:'Tawdih al-Masail, Ruling 918',tags:['adhan','iqamah','recommended','obligatory','prayer']},
{id:'s014',cat:'salat',sub:'Recitation',marja:'sistani',q:'Is it obligatory to recite prayers in Arabic?',a:'Yes, the obligatory recitations in prayer must be in Arabic. This includes: al-Fatihah and a second surah in the first two rak\'at, dhikr in ruku and sujud, tashahhud, salam, and takbirat al-ihram. A person who cannot recite Arabic must learn it. If they are unable to learn properly, they should recite as best they can. Supplications (du\'a) and qunut can be in any language, though Arabic is recommended. The qira\'ah must be pronounced correctly — significant mispronunciation that changes meaning invalidates the prayer.',source:'Minhaj al-Salihin',tags:['arabic','recitation','language','pronunciation','prayer']},
{id:'s015',cat:'salat',sub:'Recitation',marja:'makarem',q:'What should a new convert do if they cannot recite Arabic yet?',a:'A new Muslim who has not yet learned Arabic recitation should begin learning immediately. In the meantime, they should pray to the best of their ability, reciting whatever Arabic they have learned and using transliteration aids. If they can recite al-Fatihah even imperfectly, they should do so. For parts they absolutely cannot recite, they may substitute with tasbih (SubhanAllah, Alhamdulillah, La ilaha illallah, Allahu Akbar). The obligation is to learn gradually — prayer should never be abandoned simply because one cannot recite perfectly yet.',source:'Tawdih al-Masail',tags:['convert','arabic','learning','recitation','new Muslim']},
{id:'s016',cat:'salat',sub:'Miscellaneous',marja:'sistani',q:'What things invalidate prayer?',a:'Prayer is invalidated by: (1) Any of the wudu invalidators occurring during prayer. (2) Turning away from qiblah. (3) Speaking intentionally (even one word, except for answering salam). (4) Laughing intentionally and audibly. (5) Crying intentionally for worldly matters with sound. (6) Any act that breaks the form of prayer (eating, drinking, folding arms intentionally). (7) Saying "Amin" after al-Fatihah intentionally. (8) Intentionally adding or omitting a rukn (pillar) of prayer. (9) Deliberate doubt in the first two rak\'at of a 4-rak\'at prayer. (10) Facing completely away from qiblah.',source:'Tawdih al-Masail, Ruling 1135',tags:['invalidation','prayer','breaking','mubtilat']},
{id:'s017',cat:'salat',sub:'Miscellaneous',marja:'khamenei',q:'Is it permissible to pray while images of living beings are on clothing?',a:'It is a precaution to avoid praying in clothing that bears images of living beings (humans, animals), particularly large or prominent images. However, if the image is small and inconspicuous (such as a small brand logo), or is on a part of the clothing that is not visible during prayer, the prayer is valid. The prohibition is more a matter of recommended precaution (ihtiyat mustahab) for most scholars, and praying in such clothing does not definitively invalidate the prayer according to the stronger opinion.',source:'Ajwibat al-Istifta\'at',tags:['images','clothing','prayer','living beings','logo']},
{id:'s018',cat:'salat',sub:'Signs Prayer',marja:'sistani',q:'When is Salat al-Ayat (Prayer of Signs) obligatory?',a:'Salat al-Ayat becomes obligatory upon: (1) Solar eclipse — even partial. (2) Lunar eclipse — even partial. (3) Earthquake — even minor ones. (4) Any frightening natural phenomena such as severe storms, unusual thunderbolts, volcanic eruptions, tornadoes, or widespread flooding that causes general fear among people. The prayer is obligatory for all residents of the locality where the phenomenon occurs. For eclipses, the prayer must be performed during the event; for earthquakes and other events, it should be performed as soon as possible and remains an obligation until performed.',source:'Tawdih al-Masail, Ruling 1491',tags:['ayat','eclipse','earthquake','signs','natural']},
{id:'s019',cat:'salat',sub:'Eid Prayer',marja:'sistani',q:'Is Eid prayer obligatory or recommended?',a:'During the Greater Occultation of the Twelfth Imam (aj), Eid prayer (for both Eid al-Fitr and Eid al-Adha) is recommended (mustahab) and not individually obligatory. However, it is highly emphasized and attending it is one of the distinctive practices of Islam. It consists of 2 rak\'at with special additional takbirat and qunut. If performed in congregation, it is performed without adhan and iqamah. Some scholars hold that if Friday prayer is established in a city, Eid prayer gains a stronger status.',source:'Minhaj al-Salihin, Eid',tags:['eid','fitr','adha','prayer','recommended']},
{id:'s020',cat:'salat',sub:'Qada Prayer',marja:'sistani',q:'Must one make up (qada) missed prayers?',a:'Yes, making up missed obligatory prayers is obligatory. If a person misses any of the five daily prayers — whether intentionally, due to sleep, forgetfulness, or other reasons — they must make them up. There is no specific time for qada prayers; they can be performed at any time. However, one should not delay them unnecessarily. The order between qada prayers should be maintained if known (i.e., if you missed Dhuhr and Asr, pray qada Dhuhr first). A son is obligated, as a precaution, to make up the missed prayers of his deceased father (the eldest son\'s obligation).',source:'Tawdih al-Masail, Ruling 1370',tags:['qada','missed','makeup','prayer','deceased']},
{id:'s021',cat:'salat',sub:'Qada Prayer',marja:'najafi',q:'Is the eldest son obligated to make up his deceased parent\'s missed prayers?',a:'As an obligatory precaution, the eldest son is obligated to make up the missed prayers and fasts of his deceased father. This obligation applies to prayers the father missed due to valid reasons (not those he deliberately abandoned without excuse). If the son cannot perform them himself, he may hire someone to do so on his behalf. This obligation does not apply to the mother\'s missed prayers, though it is recommended. Other sons can voluntarily perform the qada on behalf of the deceased as well.',source:'Minhaj al-Salihin',tags:['eldest son','deceased','father','qada','obligation']},
{id:'s022',cat:'salat',sub:'Congregational',marja:'wahid',q:'What is the minimum distance between the imam and the follower in congregational prayer?',a:'The distance between the person praying and the person in front of them should not exceed one long step (approximately one meter). Similarly, if one is in the same row, the distance between the persons should not exceed this amount. There should be no impenetrable barrier between the imam and the followers. If praying in rows, each row must be connected to the one in front through this proximity. Excessive gaps that break the chain of connection between follower and imam invalidate the congregation for that individual.',source:'Minhaj al-Salihin',tags:['congregation','distance','row','barrier','proximity']},
{id:'s023',cat:'salat',sub:'Traveler Prayer',marja:'sistani',q:'What defines the point of tarakhkhus where a traveler begins qasr prayer?',a:'The point of tarakhkhus is where the traveler has left their city to the extent that they have passed the last houses of the city and reached a point where, as an obligatory precaution, they can neither see the walls of the city nor hear its adhan (assuming normal conditions — not blocked by mountains, etc., and not aided by amplification). At this point, they must begin praying qasr. Upon returning, they resume full prayer when they reach this point again. For modern cities, this is typically when one has exited the municipal boundaries to the point where the city is behind them.',source:'Tawdih al-Masail, Ruling 1280',tags:['tarakhkhus','travel','city','boundary','qasr']},
{id:'s024',cat:'salat',sub:'Obligatory Prayers',marja:'hamedani',q:'What is the ruling on delaying prayer until the last minutes of its time?',a:'It is obligatory to perform prayer within its designated time. Deliberately delaying prayer until just before its time expires, though technically valid, is strongly disliked (makruh shadid). The recommended practice is to perform each prayer at the beginning of its time (fadilat time), which has the greatest reward. If one delays without valid reason until the time is about to expire, the prayer is still valid but the person has committed a morally blameworthy act by disregarding the importance of prayer.',source:'Risalah Amaliyyah',tags:['delay','time','early','prayer','fadilat']},
{id:'s025',cat:'salat',sub:'Sajdah',marja:'sistani',q:'What are the seven points of prostration (sajdah)?',a:'In sajdah, seven body parts must be placed on the ground: (1) Forehead. (2) Both palms. (3) Both knees. (4) Both big toes. The forehead must be placed on a valid sajdah surface (earth, turbah, etc.). The other six parts can be placed on any surface. The forehead must be placed on the ground willingly — if it falls involuntarily, that does not count. The seven parts must all be on the ground simultaneously. The place of sajdah should not be more than four joined fingers higher or lower than where the toes are placed.',source:'Tawdih al-Masail, Ruling 1052-1063',tags:['sajdah','prostration','seven points','forehead','body']},
{id:'s026',cat:'salat',sub:'Clothing',marja:'sistani',q:'Can a man pray wearing silk clothing?',a:'It is not permissible for men to pray in pure silk clothing. Prayer performed in a garment made entirely of silk is invalid for men. This applies to the outer garments as well as undergarments. If the silk is blended with another material and the silk does not predominate, the clothing is permissible. Women are permitted to wear silk in prayer. Men are also prohibited from wearing gold jewelry during prayer — a gold ring, watch, or chain invalidates a man\'s prayer.',source:'Tawdih al-Masail, Ruling 831',tags:['silk','men','gold','clothing','prayer']},
{id:'s027',cat:'salat',sub:'Miscellaneous',marja:'makarem',q:'What is the ruling on praying with a tattoo?',a:'Tattoos do not invalidate prayer and do not create a najasah barrier. Since the ink of a tattoo is under the skin, it does not prevent water from reaching the skin during wudu or ghusl. Therefore, wudu and ghusl remain valid, and prayer performed with tattoos is valid. However, the permissibility of getting a tattoo itself is a separate question — the process of tattooing and its content should be evaluated independently. Obscene or anti-Islamic images would be problematic regardless of prayer.',source:'Tawdih al-Masail',tags:['tattoo','prayer','wudu','validity','ink']},
{id:'s028',cat:'salat',sub:'Obligatory Prayers',marja:'tabrizi',q:'What is the ruling on combining Dhuhr and Asr prayers?',a:'It is permissible to pray Dhuhr and Asr consecutively without separation, as well as Maghrib and Isha. While it is recommended (mustahab) to pray each at its specific virtuous time (fadilat), the shared time window means combining them is fully valid. The specific time for Dhuhr is from midday until the time needed for 4 rak\'at has passed; the specific time for Asr is before sunset with enough time for only 4 rak\'at. The shared time between these specific times is valid for either prayer, as long as Dhuhr is prayed first.',source:'Siraat al-Najah',tags:['combining','dhuhr','asr','maghrib','isha','time']},
{id:'s029',cat:'salat',sub:'Miscellaneous',marja:'sistani',q:'Can one pray wearing shoes?',a:'It is permissible to pray wearing shoes or sandals provided that: (1) The shoes are tahir (pure). (2) The shoes are not made from the hide of a haram animal or an animal not slaughtered Islamically. (3) The shoes are not usurped property. (4) For sujud, the big toes must be able to touch the ground. With most modern shoes, this last condition makes it impractical, so shoes are usually removed for prayer. Socks are fine as long as the toes can make contact with the ground during sujud.',source:'Tawdih al-Masail',tags:['shoes','footwear','prayer','sujud','toes']},
{id:'s030',cat:'salat',sub:'Recommended',marja:'sistani',q:'What are the recommended daily prayers (nawafil)?',a:'The daily recommended (mustahab) prayers are 34 rak\'at: (1) Fajr nafilah — 2 rak\'at before Fajr prayer. (2) Dhuhr nafilah — 8 rak\'at before Dhuhr. (3) Asr nafilah — 8 rak\'at before Asr. (4) Maghrib nafilah — 4 rak\'at after Maghrib. (5) Isha nafilah — 2 rak\'at sitting (counting as 1 rak\'at) after Isha. (6) Salat al-Layl (Tahajjud) — 11 rak\'at in the last part of the night (8 rak\'at nafilah + 2 rak\'at Shaf\' + 1 rak\'ah Witr). On Fridays, 4 extra rak\'at are added to the Dhuhr and Asr nawafil.',source:'Minhaj al-Salihin, Nawafil',tags:['nawafil','recommended','tahajjud','night prayer','daily']},
{id:'s031',cat:'salat',sub:'Congregational',marja:'sistani',q:'What are the conditions for leading congregational prayer as an imam?',a:'The imam of congregational prayer must be: (1) Baligh (mature). (2) Sane. (3) Ithna Ashari (Twelver Shia). (4) Adil (just/upright — not known to commit major sins or persist in minor ones). (5) Of legitimate birth. (6) Male (when leading men). (7) Able to perform prayer correctly — proper recitation, correct ruku, sujud, etc. (8) If he has a medical condition affecting prayer performance, the followers should not have the same condition. Additionally, the imam\'s prayer must be valid — if he is a traveler praying qasr, residents can follow him and vice versa, but adjustments must be made.',source:'Tawdih al-Masail, Ruling 1423',tags:['imam','conditions','congregational','leading']},
{id:'s032',cat:'salat',sub:'Miscellaneous',marja:'khamenei',q:'Can one pray on a moving train or bus?',a:'If the prayer time will expire before the journey ends and one cannot get off to pray, they must pray on the vehicle. They should face the qiblah and adjust if the vehicle turns. If standing is possible, they should stand; otherwise sitting is permitted. Ruku and sujud should be performed as normally as possible — even by nodding if necessary. If the prayer time is not in danger of expiring, it is better to wait until one can pray normally. For regular commuters whose prayer time frequently falls during travel, the same principles apply.',source:'Ajwibat al-Istifta\'at',tags:['train','bus','vehicle','travel','moving','prayer']},
{id:'s033',cat:'salat',sub:'Recitation',marja:'sistani',q:'Is it obligatory to recite a full surah after al-Fatihah?',a:'As an obligatory precaution, a complete surah from the Quran must be recited after al-Fatihah in the first two rak\'at of obligatory prayer. The surah should come after al-Fatihah and before ruku. It is not permissible to recite one of the two combined surahs (like al-Fil and Quraysh, or al-Duha and al-Inshirah) alone — if one recites al-Fil, they must also recite Quraysh after it, as they are considered one surah by some scholars. Short surahs like al-Ikhlas, al-Kawthar, or al-Nasr are sufficient.',source:'Tawdih al-Masail, Ruling 973',tags:['surah','recitation','fatihah','obligatory','rakat']},
{id:'s034',cat:'salat',sub:'Clothing',marja:'najafi',q:'What are the hijab requirements for women during prayer?',a:'A woman must cover her entire body during prayer except for the face (the area washed in wudu), the hands (from wrist to fingertips), and the feet (from ankle to toes). Hair must be completely covered. The covering does not need to be any specific garment — a chador, scarf with loose clothing, or any combination that covers properly is sufficient. The clothing must not be so thin that skin color shows through. If any part of the body that should be covered becomes exposed during prayer, and the person was unaware, the prayer is valid.',source:'Minhaj al-Salihin',tags:['hijab','women','covering','prayer','clothing']},
{id:'s035',cat:'salat',sub:'Miscellaneous',marja:'hakim',q:'What is the ruling on raising hands for qunut in the second rak\'ah?',a:'Qunut in the second rak\'ah of prayer (before ruku) is recommended (mustahab), not obligatory. The person raises both hands to the level of the face, palms upward, and recites any supplication. There is no specific obligatory supplication for qunut — any du\'a or dhikr is acceptable, even a simple "Rabbighfirli." Qunut is recommended in all obligatory and mustahab prayers. In Friday prayer, there is a qunut in both rak\'at. Omitting qunut does not invalidate the prayer.',source:'Misbah al-Minhaj',tags:['qunut','supplication','second rakat','raising hands']},
{id:'s036',cat:'salat',sub:'Obligatory Prayers',marja:'sistani',q:'How should a person who is physically unable to stand pray?',a:'A person who cannot stand should pray sitting. If they cannot sit, they should pray lying on their right side facing qiblah. If that is not possible, they lie on their left side. If that is not possible, they lie on their back with their feet toward the qiblah. They perform ruku and sujud by nodding — a deeper nod for sujud than for ruku. If even nodding is not possible, they indicate with their eyes (closing them for ruku and sujud). Prayer is never abandoned — it is adapted to whatever the person\'s condition allows.',source:'Tawdih al-Masail, Ruling 970',tags:['disability','sitting','lying','unable to stand','prayer']},
{id:'s037',cat:'salat',sub:'Traveler Prayer',marja:'makarem',q:'What is the ruling on prayer for a student studying in another city?',a:'A student who moves to another city for studies and intends to stay there for at least 10 days should pray full (tamam) prayers there. If they settle there for the academic period and adopt it as a temporary residence, they pray full prayers there. When traveling between their university city and hometown, they pray qasr during the journey if the distance exceeds the shar\'i limit. At their hometown (watan), they always pray full prayers. The key criterion is intention of 10-day stay or adoption of the place as a secondary residence.',source:'Tawdih al-Masail',tags:['student','university','city','qasr','tamam']},
{id:'s038',cat:'salat',sub:'Miscellaneous',marja:'sistani',q:'Is it permissible to check the time on a phone during prayer?',a:'It is not permissible to deliberately perform actions during prayer that break its form (such as picking up and looking at a phone). A brief, slight glance to the side would not invalidate prayer, but deliberately looking at a phone, picking it up, or interacting with it constitutes an act that violates the continuity and form of prayer. If one needs to know the time urgently, they should complete or end the prayer first. Phones should be silenced before prayer to avoid disruption.',source:'Istifta\'at',tags:['phone','time','checking','prayer','invalidation']},
{id:'s039',cat:'salat',sub:'Congregational',marja:'sistani',q:'Is there extra reward for congregational prayer?',a:'Yes, congregational prayer carries immensely greater reward than individual prayer. Narrations from the Ahlulbayt (as) state that a single congregational prayer is equivalent to 25 individual prayers, and some narrations mention even greater multiples. The more people in the congregation, the greater the reward. Congregational prayer for Fajr, Maghrib, and Isha is particularly emphasized. Regular attendance at congregational prayer in the mosque is one of the signs of true faith and is strongly recommended for Muslim communities.',source:'Minhaj al-Salihin',tags:['congregation','reward','merit','mosque','community']},
{id:'s040',cat:'salat',sub:'Sajdah',marja:'sistani',q:'Is prostrating on a turbah from Karbala obligatory?',a:'Prostrating on a turbah from Karbala is not obligatory — it is highly recommended (mustahab). The obligation is to prostrate on earth or things that grow from earth. A Karbala turbah fulfills this condition and adds the immense spiritual merit of connection to the soil of Imam Hussain\'s (as) martyrdom. If a turbah is not available, one can prostrate on any valid earth surface: clean soil, a rock, a leaf, wood, or paper. The turbah is the most meritorious option, not the only valid one.',source:'Tawdih al-Masail',tags:['turbah','karbala','sajdah','obligatory','recommended']},
{id:'sl041',cat:'salat',sub:'Conditions',marja:'sistani',q:'What are the conditions for the place of prayer?',a:'The place of prayer must be: (1) Usurped — one must have permission or right to pray there, (2) Stable enough to perform ruku and sujud normally, (3) The place of sujud must be level — the forehead position should not be more than four joined fingers higher or lower than the knees and toes, (4) It must be tahir where the forehead touches during sujud, (5) Women should pray behind or beside men, not in front (on obligatory precaution). Prayer in a usurped place is invalid even if just the place of sujud is usurped.',source:'Minhaj al-Salihin / Tawdih al-Masail',tags:['prayer','place','conditions','tahir','sujud']},
{id:'sl042',cat:'salat',sub:'Conditions',marja:'khamenei',q:'What are the conditions for the place of prayer?',a:'The prayer location must be owned, permitted, or public. A moving vehicle is permissible if one can perform the movements of prayer — if not, pray seated with gestures for ruku and sujud. The turbah (sajdah stone) placement must be tahir. Prayer is valid in any clean place: home, office, park, sidewalk. It is makruh to pray in a bathroom, stable, graveyard (if graves are in front), or facing a fire.',source:'Ajwibat al-Istifta\'at',tags:['prayer','place','conditions','tahir','sujud']},
{id:'sl043',cat:'salat',sub:'Conditions',marja:'makarem',q:'What are the conditions for the place of prayer?',a:'Practical guidance for the prayer location: your home, office, and public spaces are all valid. In non-Muslim countries, one may pray in any clean space. Carry a small prayer rug and turbah for convenience. On an airplane, pray in your seat if standing is not permitted — face the qiblah as much as possible. If the plane turns during prayer, adjust direction. If unsure of qiblah, pray in the most likely direction.',source:'Tawdih al-Masail / Istifta\'at',tags:['prayer','place','conditions','tahir','sujud']},
{id:'sl044',cat:'salat',sub:'Conditions',marja:'noori',q:'What are the conditions for the place of prayer?',a:'The place of prayer should ideally be a clean, quiet location conducive to concentration. While any permissible clean place is valid, praying in a mosque has exponentially more reward. The Prophet (s) said: "The earth has been made a mosque for me" — meaning Muslims can pray virtually anywhere. However, certain places are makruh or problematic: middle of a road, facing a toilet, on top of graves.',source:'Tawdih al-Masail / Istifta\'at',tags:['prayer','place','conditions','tahir','sujud']},
{id:'sl045',cat:'salat',sub:'Conditions',marja:'sistani',q:'What should one pray on (turbah/sajdah)?',a:'Sujud must be performed on earth or what grows from earth that is not eaten or worn. This includes: natural earth, stone, clay, sand, dried clay tablets (turbah), natural wood, leaves, and paper. It does NOT include: food items (rice, wheat), clothing materials (cotton when processed, linen), synthetic materials, metals, or gemstones. The turbah from Karbala is recommended due to the hadith about the merit of prostrating on the soil of Imam Hussain\'s (as) land. Prayer on carpet is NOT valid for sujud — one must use a turbah or equivalent.',source:'Minhaj al-Salihin / Tawdih al-Masail',tags:['turbah','sajdah','sujud','earth','karbala','prayer']},
{id:'sl046',cat:'salat',sub:'Conditions',marja:'khamenei',q:'What should one pray on (turbah/sajdah)?',a:'Valid sujud surfaces: natural earth, stone, sand, pebbles, wooden boards, paper, leaves. The turbah (clay tablet) is the most common solution — carry one at all times. If a turbah is unavailable, pray on paper (a piece of cardboard, a newspaper), clean stone, or any natural surface. Cotton clothing can be used as a last resort if absolutely nothing else is available. The turbah from Karbala has special significance but is not obligatory.',source:'Ajwibat al-Istifta\'at',tags:['turbah','sajdah','sujud','earth','karbala','prayer']},
{id:'sl047',cat:'salat',sub:'Conditions',marja:'makarem',q:'What should one pray on (turbah/sajdah)?',a:'The sujud surface must be from earth or its products (not eaten/worn). In practice, most Shias use a turbah (mohr). If you forget your turbah: a clean napkin or tissue (made from wood pulp = from earth), a piece of paper, a clean stone, or even a leaf will work. Some scholars permit sujud on clean cotton fabric as a last resort. Marble and granite floors are valid since they are stone.',source:'Tawdih al-Masail / Istifta\'at',tags:['turbah','sajdah','sujud','earth','karbala','prayer']},
{id:'sl048',cat:'salat',sub:'Conditions',marja:'shirazi',q:'What should one pray on (turbah/sajdah)?',a:'The ruling on sajdah surfaces is based on numerous hadiths emphasizing humility before Allah — prostrating on the earth itself represents the ultimate submission. The soil of Karbala is particularly meritorious due to its association with Imam Hussain\'s (as) sacrifice. Modern turbah is typically made from Karbala clay, but any clean earth, stone, or unprocessed plant material is valid.',source:'al-Fiqh Series / Tawdih al-Masail',tags:['turbah','sajdah','sujud','earth','karbala','prayer']},
{id:'sl049',cat:'salat',sub:'Daily Prayers',marja:'sistani',q:'What are the times for each daily prayer?',a:'The five daily prayers and their times: (1) Fajr: from true dawn (fajr al-sadiq) until sunrise — best time is early. (2) Dhuhr: from the sun passing its zenith until there is time only for Asr — approximately midday. (3) Asr: after Dhuhr time, until sunset — can be prayed immediately after Dhuhr. (4) Maghrib: after sunset when the eastern redness passes overhead, until midnight — best immediately after sunset. (5) Isha: after Maghrib time, until midnight (religious midnight = halfway between sunset and fajr). In Shia practice, Dhuhr and Asr can be combined, as can Maghrib and Isha.',source:'Minhaj al-Salihin / Tawdih al-Masail',tags:['prayer','times','daily','fajr','dhuhr','asr','maghrib','isha']},
{id:'sl050',cat:'salat',sub:'Daily Prayers',marja:'khamenei',q:'What are the times for each daily prayer?',a:'Prayer times are astronomical and vary by location and season. The combining of Dhuhr-Asr and Maghrib-Isha is a distinctive practice of Shia Muslims, based on hadith showing the Prophet (s) combined prayers even without travel. This means practically: pray Dhuhr then immediately Asr, and Maghrib then immediately Isha. Having separate times is recommended (mustahab) but not obligatory. Friday prayer has its own special time around noon.',source:'Ajwibat al-Istifta\'at',tags:['prayer','times','daily','fajr','dhuhr','asr','maghrib','isha']},
{id:'sl051',cat:'salat',sub:'Daily Prayers',marja:'makarem',q:'What are the times for each daily prayer?',a:'Practical prayer timing: Fajr — check your local Islamic prayer time app; it varies significantly by season and latitude. Dhuhr/Asr — can be prayed together anytime from noon to sunset. Maghrib/Isha — can be prayed together from after sunset to religious midnight. The combining of prayers is one of the practical allowances in Shia fiqh based on authentic hadith. It is recommended but not required to separate them.',source:'Tawdih al-Masail / Istifta\'at',tags:['prayer','times','daily','fajr','dhuhr','asr','maghrib','isha']},
{id:'sl052',cat:'salat',sub:'Daily Prayers',marja:'sistani',q:'How many rak\'ahs are in each daily prayer?',a:'The daily prayers total 17 rak\'ahs: (1) Fajr: 2 rak\'ahs, (2) Dhuhr: 4 rak\'ahs, (3) Asr: 4 rak\'ahs, (4) Maghrib: 3 rak\'ahs, (5) Isha: 4 rak\'ahs. During travel (when the distance and conditions meet the travel criteria), the 4-rak\'ah prayers are shortened to 2 rak\'ahs each. Fajr and Maghrib are never shortened. The daily nawafil (recommended prayers) total 34 rak\'ahs but are not obligatory.',source:'Minhaj al-Salihin / Tawdih al-Masail',tags:['prayer','rakahs','daily','number','travel']},
{id:'sl053',cat:'salat',sub:'Daily Prayers',marja:'khamenei',q:'How many rak\'ahs are in each daily prayer?',a:'The 17 daily rak\'ahs are: Fajr 2, Dhuhr 4, Asr 4, Maghrib 3, Isha 4. For travelers meeting the travel distance (approximately 44 km one way), the 4-rak\'ah prayers become 2 rak\'ahs. A traveler prays: 2+2+2+3+2 = 11 rak\'ahs total. The recommended nawafil are: 2 before Fajr, 8 before Dhuhr, 8 before Asr, 4 after Maghrib, 2 after Isha, and 11 for night prayer (salat al-layl).',source:'Ajwibat al-Istifta\'at',tags:['prayer','rakahs','daily','number','travel']},
{id:'sl054',cat:'salat',sub:'Qiblah',marja:'sistani',q:'How should one determine the qiblah direction?',a:'One must face the Ka\'bah in Mecca for prayer. Methods to determine qiblah: (1) Direct knowledge — if you can see the Ka\'bah. (2) Reliable information — compass, qiblah apps, asking trustworthy knowledgeable people. (3) Scientific methods — using the sun\'s position, stars, or maps. (4) If all methods are exhausted and doubt remains, pray in the direction you consider most likely. If equally doubtful between two directions, on obligatory precaution, pray in both directions. Modern smartphone qiblah apps are sufficient for determining direction.',source:'Minhaj al-Salihin / Tawdih al-Masail',tags:['qiblah','direction','prayer','compass','kaaba']},
{id:'sl055',cat:'salat',sub:'Qiblah',marja:'khamenei',q:'How should one determine the qiblah direction?',a:'Modern technology has made qiblah determination easy — smartphone apps using GPS and compass are reliable and sufficient. For those without technology: the sun rises in the east and sets in the west; in the northern hemisphere, Mecca is generally southeast to south depending on your location. Ask local Muslims or check the direction of local mosques. If completely uncertain after effort, pray in the most probable direction — Allah does not burden a soul beyond its capacity.',source:'Ajwibat al-Istifta\'at',tags:['qiblah','direction','prayer','compass','kaaba']},
{id:'sl056',cat:'salat',sub:'Qiblah',marja:'makarem',q:'How should one determine the qiblah direction?',a:'In today\'s world, multiple qiblah apps provide accurate direction. When traveling: download a qiblah app before your trip. In a new building: check multiple apps for confirmation. The qiblah requirement is to face the general direction — not a precise laser-like alignment. A reasonable effort to face the correct direction is sufficient. If you later discover you prayed in a significantly wrong direction, the prayer should be repeated.',source:'Tawdih al-Masail / Istifta\'at',tags:['qiblah','direction','prayer','compass','kaaba']},
{id:'sl057',cat:'salat',sub:'Traveler Prayer',marja:'sistani',q:'When does a traveler shorten their prayers?',a:'A traveler (musafir) shortens 4-rak\'ah prayers to 2 rak\'ahs when: (1) The travel distance is at least 8 farsakh (approximately 44 km/27 miles) one-way, or 4 farsakh with return planned, (2) The intention to travel this distance exists from the start, (3) The traveler does not intend to stay at the destination for 10+ days, (4) The travel is not for sinful purpose, (5) The traveler is not a professional traveler (whose work IS traveling — they pray full). The distance is measured from the city limits, not from one\'s home.',source:'Minhaj al-Salihin / Tawdih al-Masail',tags:['travel','qasr','shortened','distance','prayer','musafir']},
{id:'sl058',cat:'salat',sub:'Traveler Prayer',marja:'khamenei',q:'When does a traveler shorten their prayers?',a:'The travel prayer (qasr) conditions include the distance threshold of approximately 44 km. Important modern scenarios: daily commuters who travel 44+ km to work should check if they qualify as "professional travelers" who pray full. If one\'s journey is uncertain in distance, pray full until certain of meeting the threshold. At the destination: if you plan to stay 10 days, pray full from day one. If less than 10 days, continue shortened prayers.',source:'Ajwibat al-Istifta\'at',tags:['travel','qasr','shortened','distance','prayer','musafir']},
{id:'sl059',cat:'salat',sub:'Traveler Prayer',marja:'makarem',q:'When does a traveler shorten their prayers?',a:'Practical travel prayer scenarios: (1) Business trip to a city 100km away for 3 days — shortened prayers entire trip. (2) Moving to a new city permanently — full prayers from arrival. (3) College student in a dorm — if the semester is 10+ days, full prayers at the college location. (4) Commuting 50km daily for work — this is debated; if it\'s your regular occupation, most scholars say pray full at work. (5) Road trip with multiple stops — shortened as long as you don\'t stay 10 days anywhere.',source:'Tawdih al-Masail / Istifta\'at',tags:['travel','qasr','shortened','distance','prayer','musafir']},
{id:'sl060',cat:'salat',sub:'Traveler Prayer',marja:'safi',q:'When does a traveler shorten their prayers?',a:'The qasr prayer is a definite ruling that cannot be ignored — it is not an option but an obligation during travel. Praying full prayers when shortened is required (with knowledge and intention) invalidates the prayer. This strictness shows the importance of this ruling. The conditions must be carefully evaluated, especially for modern lifestyles with frequent travel.',source:'Hidayat al-Ibad / Tawdih al-Masail',tags:['travel','qasr','shortened','distance','prayer','musafir']},
{id:'sl061',cat:'salat',sub:'Congregational Prayer',marja:'sistani',q:'What is the merit of praying in congregation (jama\'ah)?',a:'Congregational prayer is highly recommended (mustahab mu\'akkad) for all obligatory prayers, especially for neighbors of the mosque. The reward multiplies exponentially: 2 people = 150x reward, 3 people = 600x, and it keeps multiplying. Conditions for the imam: must be male (for leading men), just (adil), have correct recitation, and be of legitimate birth. The followers (ma\'mum) stand behind the imam in rows. If only one follower, they stand to the right of the imam.',source:'Minhaj al-Salihin / Tawdih al-Masail',tags:['congregation','jamaah','imam','mosque','reward','prayer']},
{id:'sl062',cat:'salat',sub:'Congregational Prayer',marja:'khamenei',q:'What is the merit of praying in congregation (jama\'ah)?',a:'Jama\'ah prayer has immense spiritual and social value. The Prophet (s) said: "Prayer in congregation is twenty-seven times better than prayer alone." It builds community bonds, demonstrates Muslim unity, and helps maintain regular prayer habits. Women may lead other women in congregation. Mixed-gender congregations are led by men, with women standing in rows behind men. Friday prayer (Salat al-Jumu\'ah) is the most important congregational prayer.',source:'Ajwibat al-Istifta\'at',tags:['congregation','jamaah','imam','mosque','reward','prayer']},
{id:'sl063',cat:'salat',sub:'Congregational Prayer',marja:'noori',q:'What is the merit of praying in congregation (jama\'ah)?',a:'The emphasis on congregational prayer in hadith is extraordinary — it is among the most recommended acts in Islam. Attending the mosque for jama\'ah prayer connects one with the community, strengthens faith through collective worship, and creates accountability for maintaining prayer times. In Shia tradition, praying behind a just and knowledgeable imam amplifies the spiritual benefit. Children should be brought to the mosque to develop the habit early.',source:'Tawdih al-Masail / Istifta\'at',tags:['congregation','jamaah','imam','mosque','reward','prayer']},
{id:'sl064',cat:'salat',sub:'Sujud',marja:'sistani',q:'What is said during sujud (prostration)?',a:'During sujud, one must say at minimum once: "Subhana Rabbi al-A\'la wa bihamdih" (Glory to my Lord the Most High, and praise be to Him). Saying it three times is recommended. Seven parts of the body must touch the ground simultaneously during sujud: (1) Forehead (on turbah or valid surface), (2-3) Both palms, (4-5) Both knees, (6-7) Both big toes. The forehead placement must be on earth or what grows from earth (not eaten/worn).',source:'Minhaj al-Salihin / Tawdih al-Masail',tags:['sujud','prostration','dhikr','prayer','forehead']},
{id:'sl065',cat:'salat',sub:'Sujud',marja:'khamenei',q:'What is said during sujud (prostration)?',a:'The dhikr of sujud is: "Subhana Rabbi al-A\'la wa bihamdih" — once is obligatory, three times is recommended. One may also say "Subhanallah" three times as a minimum. Sujud is the closest position a servant has to Allah — the Prophet (s) said: "The closest a servant is to his Lord is when he is in prostration." It is recommended to prolong sujud and make extensive du\'a in this position.',source:'Ajwibat al-Istifta\'at',tags:['sujud','prostration','dhikr','prayer','forehead']},
{id:'sl066',cat:'salat',sub:'Sujud',marja:'makarem',q:'What is said during sujud (prostration)?',a:'Sujud is the most powerful moment in prayer — take advantage of it for du\'a after completing the obligatory dhikr. The seven body parts must all be touching their surfaces simultaneously. Common mistakes: lifting toes during sujud, not placing the forehead firmly on the turbah, or rushing through the dhikr before the body is settled. One should be still and calm in sujud before beginning the dhikr.',source:'Tawdih al-Masail / Istifta\'at',tags:['sujud','prostration','dhikr','prayer','forehead']},
{id:'sl067',cat:'salat',sub:'Mistakes',marja:'sistani',q:'What happens if one makes a mistake during prayer?',a:'The rules for mistakes in prayer are detailed: (1) If one forgets a rukn (pillar) and remembers before entering the next rukn, return and perform it. If remembered after entering the next rukn, the prayer is void (except for the extra rukn of ruku or sujud which are forgiven). (2) For non-pillar elements, if forgotten and the place has passed, continue — it does not void the prayer. (3) Doubt in the number of rak\'ahs in 4-rak\'ah prayers has specific resolution rules (prayers of ihtiyat). (4) The sajdah of sahw (forgetfulness) is required for certain mistakes.',source:'Minhaj al-Salihin / Tawdih al-Masail',tags:['mistakes','prayer','sahw','doubt','rukn','correction']},
{id:'sl068',cat:'salat',sub:'Mistakes',marja:'khamenei',q:'What happens if one makes a mistake during prayer?',a:'Common prayer mistakes and solutions: forgot al-Fatiha but remembered during the next surah — go back and recite it. Forgot ruku and already entered sujud — prayer is void, restart. Unsure if 3rd or 4th rak\'ah — assume 4th and pray one rak\'ah of ihtiyat prayer afterward. Accidentally added a rak\'ah — if remembered during, sit down and complete tashahhud. Sajdah al-sahw compensates for minor errors: two additional sujuds after the prayer with specific dhikr.',source:'Ajwibat al-Istifta\'at',tags:['mistakes','prayer','sahw','doubt','rukn','correction']},
{id:'sl069',cat:'salat',sub:'Mistakes',marja:'makarem',q:'What happens if one makes a mistake during prayer?',a:'A practical summary: pillars of prayer (arkan) are: niyyah, takbiratul ihram, standing (qiyam), ruku, and two sujuds. Adding or omitting a rukn voids the prayer. Non-pillar parts (qunut, first tashahhud, one sujud if corrected) don\'t void prayer if forgotten. For rak\'ah count doubts in the last two rak\'ahs, assume the higher number and add ihtiyat prayer. Frequent doubters (kathir al-shakk) should ignore their doubts entirely.',source:'Tawdih al-Masail / Istifta\'at',tags:['mistakes','prayer','sahw','doubt','rukn','correction']},
{id:'sl070',cat:'salat',sub:'Friday Prayer',marja:'sistani',q:'What is the ruling on Friday prayer (Salat al-Jumu\'ah)?',a:'Salat al-Jumu\'ah during the occultation of the 12th Imam (as) is wajib takhyiri — meaning one has the choice between praying Jumu\'ah (2 rak\'ahs with 2 khutbahs) or praying regular Dhuhr (4 rak\'ahs). If one prays Jumu\'ah properly, it suffices for Dhuhr. Conditions: minimum 5 participants including the imam, two khutbahs before the prayer, the imam must be just and qualified, minimum distance between two Jumu\'ah prayers (approximately 5.5 km).',source:'Minhaj al-Salihin / Tawdih al-Masail',tags:['friday','jumuah','prayer','khutbah','congregation']},
{id:'sl071',cat:'salat',sub:'Friday Prayer',marja:'khamenei',q:'What is the ruling on Friday prayer (Salat al-Jumu\'ah)?',a:'Friday prayer is obligatory (wajib ayni) when led by a qualified imam and conditions are met. The two sermons (khutbahs) are essential components — they include praise of Allah, salawat, and addressing community issues. Friday prayer is a powerful institution for Muslim unity, political awareness, and social cohesion. Attending Friday prayer is one of the most important weekly obligations.',source:'Ajwibat al-Istifta\'at',tags:['friday','jumuah','prayer','khutbah','congregation']},
{id:'sl072',cat:'salat',sub:'Friday Prayer',marja:'makarem',q:'What is the ruling on Friday prayer (Salat al-Jumu\'ah)?',a:'Friday prayer is wajib takhyiri — one must pray either Jumu\'ah or Dhuhr. If Jumu\'ah is established in your area with proper conditions, attending is highly recommended and possibly obligatory. The two khutbahs replace two rak\'ahs — so Jumu\'ah is 2 rak\'ahs instead of Dhuhr\'s 4. It\'s a time for community gathering, Islamic education through the khutbahs, and strengthening Muslim brotherhood.',source:'Tawdih al-Masail / Istifta\'at',tags:['friday','jumuah','prayer','khutbah','congregation']},
{id:'sl073',cat:'salat',sub:'Nawafil',marja:'sistani',q:'What are the recommended daily prayers (nawafil)?',a:'The daily nawafil (supererogatory prayers) are: (1) Nafilah of Fajr: 2 rak\'ahs before Fajr prayer, (2) Nafilah of Dhuhr: 8 rak\'ahs (4x2) before Dhuhr, (3) Nafilah of Asr: 8 rak\'ahs (4x2) before Asr, (4) Nafilah of Maghrib: 4 rak\'ahs (2x2) after Maghrib, (5) Nafilah of Isha: 2 rak\'ahs sitting (counted as 1 rak\'ah), (6) Salat al-Layl (night prayer): 11 rak\'ahs in the last third of the night. Total: 34 rak\'ahs. These are dropped during travel except Fajr nafilah and Salat al-Layl.',source:'Minhaj al-Salihin / Tawdih al-Masail',tags:['nawafil','recommended','prayer','night','salat al-layl']},
{id:'sl074',cat:'salat',sub:'Nawafil',marja:'khamenei',q:'What are the recommended daily prayers (nawafil)?',a:'Nawafil are among the most important acts of worship for spiritual growth. The night prayer (salat al-layl) is particularly emphasized — numerous hadiths describe its transformative effect on the soul. It consists of 8 rak\'ahs in pairs, then 2 rak\'ahs of Shaf\', then 1 rak\'ah of Witr. The best time is the last third of the night. If unable to wake up, it can be prayed before sleeping. The Quran says: "Stand in prayer at night — it is more effective for contemplation" (73:6).',source:'Ajwibat al-Istifta\'at',tags:['nawafil','recommended','prayer','night','salat al-layl']},
{id:'sl075',cat:'salat',sub:'Qada',marja:'sistani',q:'How should missed prayers (qada) be made up?',a:'Missed obligatory prayers must be made up (qada) as soon as reasonably possible. Rules: (1) Order is not required between qada prayers of different days, but within the same day\'s prayers, maintaining order is obligatory precaution. (2) A son is obligated to make up his deceased father\'s missed prayers (the eldest son). (3) One can hire someone to perform qada prayers for a deceased person. (4) Qada can be prayed at any time — there is no restricted time. (5) The traveler\'s qada follows the original missed prayer — if missed during travel, pray 2 rak\'ahs; if missed at home, pray 4.',source:'Minhaj al-Salihin / Tawdih al-Masail',tags:['qada','missed','prayers','makeup','obligation','eldest son']},
{id:'sl076',cat:'salat',sub:'Qada',marja:'makarem',q:'How should missed prayers (qada) be made up?',a:'Missing prayers deliberately is a major sin. Making them up is obligatory. Practical advice for those with many qada prayers: (1) Pray them consistently — even one set (5 prayers) per day adds up. (2) Combine with daily prayers — pray a qada before or after each daily prayer. (3) The eldest son must fulfill his deceased father\'s qada obligations — this can be done personally or by hiring someone. (4) Keep a count of estimated missed prayers and work through them systematically.',source:'Tawdih al-Masail / Istifta\'at',tags:['qada','missed','prayers','makeup','obligation','eldest son']},
{id:'sl077',cat:'salat',sub:'Qada',marja:'khamenei',q:'How should missed prayers (qada) be made up?',a:'Qada prayers are a debt to Allah that must be fulfilled. There is no statute of limitations — prayers missed years ago still need to be made up. For someone with many missed prayers: start by estimating the total and create a plan. Even if the exact number is uncertain, pray until you are reasonably confident all have been covered. The reward of qada prayers is immense, and their neglect carries serious spiritual consequence.',source:'Ajwibat al-Istifta\'at',tags:['qada','missed','prayers','makeup','obligation','eldest son']},
{id:'sl078',cat:'salat',sub:'Special Prayers',marja:'sistani',q:'What is Salat al-Ayat (prayer for natural phenomena)?',a:'Salat al-Ayat is obligatory upon: (1) Solar eclipse (partial or full), (2) Lunar eclipse (partial or full), (3) Earthquake, (4) Thunder, lightning, or any natural phenomenon that causes widespread fear. It consists of 2 rak\'ahs, each containing 5 ruku\'s. Method: Takbir, recite al-Fatiha and a surah, perform ruku, stand, recite Fatiha and surah, ruku, stand — repeat 5 times total, then 2 sujuds. It can also be done by splitting one surah across the 5 standings.',source:'Minhaj al-Salihin / Tawdih al-Masail',tags:['ayat','eclipse','earthquake','special','prayer','natural']},
{id:'sl079',cat:'salat',sub:'Special Prayers',marja:'khamenei',q:'What is Salat al-Ayat (prayer for natural phenomena)?',a:'Salat al-Ayat reminds believers that natural events are signs (ayat) of Allah\'s power. It is obligatory for anyone who witnesses or becomes aware of the triggering event while it is occurring (for eclipses) or immediately after (for earthquakes). Each of the 2 rak\'ahs has 5 ruku\'s — making it 10 ruku\'s total. If one was unaware of an eclipse until after it ended, they should still pray it as soon as they learn of it.',source:'Ajwibat al-Istifta\'at',tags:['ayat','eclipse','earthquake','special','prayer','natural']},
{id:'sl080',cat:'salat',sub:'Special Prayers',marja:'makarem',q:'What is Salat al-Ayat (prayer for natural phenomena)?',a:'This unique prayer demonstrates Islam\'s connection between worship and the natural world. Modern context: check astronomy apps for eclipse schedules so you can prepare. For earthquakes: pray as soon as safety allows. The prayer is not restricted to any specific time and can be performed individually. It is a powerful reminder that we are surrounded by divine signs and should respond with humility and worship.',source:'Tawdih al-Masail / Istifta\'at',tags:['ayat','eclipse','earthquake','special','prayer','natural']},

{id:'sl081',cat:'salat',sub:'Traveler Prayer',marja:'sistani',q:'What is the ruling on prayer while traveling?',a:'A traveler (musafir) must shorten 4-rak\'ah prayers to 2 rak\'ahs (qasr) when traveling 44km or more (approximately 8 farsakhs). Fajr (2 rak\'ah) and Maghrib (3 rak\'ah) are not shortened. Conditions: the journey must be intentional, not sinful in purpose, the person must not be a frequent traveler by profession, and they must travel beyond the city limits. In their hometown, full prayers are offered.',source:'Minhaj al-Salihin / Tawdih al-Masail',tags:['travel','qasr','prayer','shortened','distance']},
{id:'sl082',cat:'salat',sub:'Traveler Prayer',marja:'khamenei',q:'What is the ruling on prayer while traveling?',a:'Qasr (shortened) prayer applies when traveling 44km or more. The four-rak\'ah prayers (Dhuhr, Asr, Isha) become two rak\'ahs. If one stays in a location for 10 days or more, they revert to full prayers from the first day of their intended stay. Frequent travelers (truck drivers, airline crew) pray full prayers as their travel is their norm.',source:'Ajwibat al-Istifta\'at',tags:['travel','qasr','prayer','shortened','distance']},
{id:'sl083',cat:'salat',sub:'Traveler Prayer',marja:'makarem',q:'What is the ruling on prayer while traveling?',a:'The traveler\'s prayer is a concession (rukhsah) from Allah. Distance threshold is approximately 44km one way. The traveler also does not fast during Ramadan but must make up missed fasts later. If one is uncertain whether their journey reaches the required distance, they pray full. When in doubt about length of stay, qasr continues until 10 days of definite stay is intended.',source:'Tawdih al-Masail / Istifta\'at',tags:['travel','qasr','prayer','shortened','distance']},
{id:'sl084',cat:'salat',sub:'Traveler Prayer',marja:'sistani',q:'What is the 10-day rule for travelers?',a:'If a traveler intends to stay in one location for 10 complete days, they must pray full prayers (tamam) from the beginning of their stay. If they are unsure or plan to stay less than 10 days, they pray qasr. If they initially planned less but later decide to extend to 10 days, they switch to full prayers from the moment of that decision. After completing 10 days, if they travel again, qasr resumes.',source:'Minhaj al-Salihin / Tawdih al-Masail',tags:['travel','ten days','stay','qasr','tamam']},
{id:'sl085',cat:'salat',sub:'Traveler Prayer',marja:'khamenei',q:'What is the 10-day rule for travelers?',a:'The 10-day rule: once a traveler firmly intends to stay 10 continuous days in one place, full prayers apply from day one. The intention must be definite — "maybe" or "let\'s see" does not count. If one offers a single full 4-rak\'ah prayer in the location, all subsequent prayers must also be full for the duration of that stay. Weekend trips home don\'t restart the count if one returns to the same place.',source:'Ajwibat al-Istifta\'at',tags:['travel','ten days','stay','qasr','tamam']},
{id:'sl086',cat:'salat',sub:'Qibla',marja:'sistani',q:'How should one determine the Qibla direction?',a:'One must face the Ka\'bah in Mecca during prayer. Methods to determine Qibla: (1) Reliable compass or smartphone app, (2) Asking trustworthy local Muslims, (3) Mihrab in established mosques, (4) Scientific calculation based on coordinates. If none available, one must make their best effort — prayer toward the approximate direction is valid. If genuinely unable to determine, pray in all four directions. The degree of precision required is reasonable direction, not exact pinpoint accuracy.',source:'Minhaj al-Salihin / Tawdih al-Masail',tags:['qibla','direction','compass','prayer','mecca']},
{id:'sl087',cat:'salat',sub:'Qibla',marja:'khamenei',q:'How should one determine the Qibla direction?',a:'Qibla determination can use modern technology — compass apps are valid and reliable. The requirement is to face the general direction of Ka\'bah, not its exact point. In large congregational settings, following the established mosque Qibla is sufficient. If one prays and later discovers the direction was significantly wrong, the prayer should be repeated if still within the prayer time.',source:'Ajwibat al-Istifta\'at',tags:['qibla','direction','compass','prayer','mecca']},
{id:'sl088',cat:'salat',sub:'Qibla',marja:'makarem',q:'How should one determine the Qibla direction?',a:'Modern technology has made Qibla determination easy. Compass apps, GPS-based Qibla finders, and Google Maps are all acceptable tools. The obligation is reasonable accuracy — the exact bearing to the degree is not required. In the Southern hemisphere, North-Northeast is generally correct. When absolutely uncertain, pray in the direction you believe is most likely correct.',source:'Tawdih al-Masail / Istifta\'at',tags:['qibla','direction','compass','prayer','mecca']},
{id:'sl089',cat:'salat',sub:'Prayer Times',marja:'sistani',q:'Can prayers be combined?',a:'Yes, it is permissible to combine Dhuhr with Asr (praying them consecutively) and Maghrib with Isha (praying them consecutively). This is one of the distinctive practices in Shia Islam based on authentic hadith. While it is mustahab to pray each at its preferred time, combining is absolutely valid and the Prophet (s) himself combined prayers without travel or rain. There is no requirement to separate them.',source:'Minhaj al-Salihin / Tawdih al-Masail',tags:['combining','prayers','dhuhr','asr','maghrib','isha']},
{id:'sl090',cat:'salat',sub:'Prayer Times',marja:'khamenei',q:'Can prayers be combined?',a:'Combining prayers is permissible at all times — whether traveling or at home. Dhuhr-Asr can be prayed together, and Maghrib-Isha can be prayed together. The separate preferred times exist for extra virtue (fadilah), but combining is fully valid. This practice is well-established in Shia fiqh based on hadith narrated even in Sunni sources.',source:'Ajwibat al-Istifta\'at',tags:['combining','prayers','dhuhr','asr','maghrib','isha']},
{id:'sl091',cat:'salat',sub:'Prayer Times',marja:'shirazi',q:'Can prayers be combined?',a:'The permissibility of combining prayers is a hallmark of Shia practice based on numerous authentic narrations. The Prophet (s) combined prayers to demonstrate its permissibility and ease for the ummah. While scholars recommend praying at the preferred (fadilah) times for maximum reward, combining is never problematic and the prayer is fully valid.',source:'al-Fiqh Series / Tawdih al-Masail',tags:['combining','prayers','dhuhr','asr','maghrib','isha']},
{id:'sl092',cat:'salat',sub:'Prostration',marja:'sistani',q:'On what surfaces can one perform sajdah?',a:'Sajdah must be performed on earth or what grows from it (not eaten as food or worn as clothing). Valid surfaces: earth, sand, stone, clay tablets (turbah), wood, paper, leaves, grass (that is not normally eaten). Invalid: carpet, synthetic materials, metals, food items (wheat, rice), cloth (cotton while on the plant is valid, but woven cotton is not). The turbah of Karbala is the most recommended surface.',source:'Minhaj al-Salihin / Tawdih al-Masail',tags:['sajdah','prostration','turbah','surface','earth']},
{id:'sl093',cat:'salat',sub:'Prostration',marja:'khamenei',q:'On what surfaces can one perform sajdah?',a:'Valid sajdah surfaces include: natural earth, stone (including marble), wood, paper, and plants not normally eaten or worn. A turbah (prayer tablet) from Karbala clay is the best choice. Invalid surfaces: all fabrics, metals, plastics, edible plants (fruits, vegetables, grains), and items normally worn (cotton fabric, linen). In emergency (plane, hospital), one can use paper or the back of a wooden object.',source:'Ajwibat al-Istifta\'at',tags:['sajdah','prostration','turbah','surface','earth']},
{id:'sl094',cat:'salat',sub:'Prostration',marja:'makarem',q:'On what surfaces can one perform sajdah?',a:'Sajdah surfaces follow the principle of what comes from the earth but is not food or clothing material. Earth, sand, rock, dried leaves, unprocessed wood, and paper are all valid. The turbah is recommended because it reminds the worshipper of Imam Hussain\'s sacrifice and represents the earth in its purest form. In dire necessity where nothing valid is available, sajdah on one\'s clothing is a last resort.',source:'Tawdih al-Masail / Istifta\'at',tags:['sajdah','prostration','turbah','surface','earth']},
{id:'sl095',cat:'salat',sub:'Jama\'ah',marja:'sistani',q:'What are the benefits of congregational prayer?',a:'Congregational prayer (salat al-jama\'ah) carries immense reward — hadith mention that one rak\'ah in congregation equals 150 rak\'ahs prayed individually, increasing with more congregants. It is highly recommended (mustahab mu\'akkad) and some scholars consider it wajib for certain prayers. Conditions: the imam must be just (adil), male for obligatory prayers, and a mujtahid or acting on taqlid. The congregation strengthens community bonds and Islamic solidarity.',source:'Minhaj al-Salihin / Tawdih al-Masail',tags:['congregational','jamaah','reward','imam','community']},
{id:'sl096',cat:'salat',sub:'Jama\'ah',marja:'khamenei',q:'What are the benefits of congregational prayer?',a:'Salat al-jama\'ah is among the most emphasized mustahab acts in Islam. The Prophet (s) said if people knew its reward, they would attend even if crawling. Benefits: spiritual multiplication of reward, community unity, demonstration of Islamic brotherhood, encouragement for the weak in faith, and establishing the visible presence of Islam. Attending is so important that leaving it is considered by some narrations as a sign of hypocrisy.',source:'Ajwibat al-Istifta\'at',tags:['congregational','jamaah','reward','imam','community']},
{id:'sl097',cat:'salat',sub:'Qada',marja:'sistani',q:'How should missed (qada) prayers be performed?',a:'Missed obligatory prayers must be made up (qada) as soon as possible. They are performed in the same format as the original — if Dhuhr was missed, pray 4 rak\'ahs. The order between different qada prayers is not obligatory, though maintaining the original order is recommended. If one has many years of missed prayers, they should estimate the number and work through them consistently. Qada can be prayed at any time — there is no restriction on when.',source:'Minhaj al-Salihin / Tawdih al-Masail',tags:['qada','missed','prayer','makeup','obligation']},
{id:'sl098',cat:'salat',sub:'Qada',marja:'khamenei',q:'How should missed (qada) prayers be performed?',a:'Qada prayers are obligatory for any missed obligatory prayer (except those waived during menstruation). One should prioritize making them up without unnecessary delay. There is no specific order required between different qada prayers, but praying that day\'s missed prayers before the current day\'s prayers is recommended. If one owes years of prayers, consistent daily effort to clear the backlog is important.',source:'Ajwibat al-Istifta\'at',tags:['qada','missed','prayer','makeup','obligation']},
{id:'sl099',cat:'salat',sub:'Qada',marja:'noori',q:'How should missed (qada) prayers be performed?',a:'Missed prayers carry a heavy obligation. The scholars emphasize beginning qada immediately and maintaining consistency. Some recommend pairing qada with ada (current) prayers — for example, praying one qada Dhuhr before each daily Dhuhr. If the exact number is unknown, continue until one is reasonably certain the obligation is fulfilled. A child\'s or parent\'s unfulfilled prayers may also need to be performed by the eldest son.',source:'Tawdih al-Masail / Istifta\'at',tags:['qada','missed','prayer','makeup','obligation']},
{id:'sl100',cat:'salat',sub:'Friday Prayer',marja:'sistani',q:'What is the ruling on Friday prayer (Salat al-Jumu\'ah)?',a:'Friday prayer is wajib takhyiri during the occultation — meaning one has the choice between Jumu\'ah prayer and regular Dhuhr prayer. If Jumu\'ah is established by a qualified imam and one attends, it replaces Dhuhr. It consists of 2 rak\'ahs preceded by two khutbahs (sermons). Minimum attendance is 5 people including the imam. The imam must be just, knowledgeable, and ideally appointed or authorized.',source:'Minhaj al-Salihin / Tawdih al-Masail',tags:['friday','jumuah','prayer','khutbah','congregation']},
{id:'sl101',cat:'salat',sub:'Friday Prayer',marja:'khamenei',q:'What is the ruling on Friday prayer (Salat al-Jumu\'ah)?',a:'Jumu\'ah prayer is wajib takhyiri — obligatory with the option of Dhuhr as alternative. However, attending Jumu\'ah is strongly emphasized and preferred. In the Islamic Republic, Friday prayers are established nationwide with designated imams. The two sermons are integral parts — they should address both religious guidance and current affairs of the Muslim community. Attending Jumu\'ah builds community cohesion.',source:'Ajwibat al-Istifta\'at',tags:['friday','jumuah','prayer','khutbah','congregation']},
{id:'sl102',cat:'salat',sub:'Friday Prayer',marja:'makarem',q:'What is the ruling on Friday prayer (Salat al-Jumu\'ah)?',a:'Friday prayer replaces Dhuhr when properly established. Its conditions include: qualified imam, minimum congregation, and proper sermons covering Islamic teachings and community affairs. While technically one can pray Dhuhr instead during occultation, the communal, spiritual, and social benefits of Jumu\'ah make attendance highly preferred. Women are not obligated to attend but may do so.',source:'Tawdih al-Masail / Istifta\'at',tags:['friday','jumuah','prayer','khutbah','congregation']},

{id:'sl103',cat:'salat',sub:'Dhikr',marja:'sistani',q:'What are the obligatory dhikr in prayer?',a:'Obligatory adhkar include: (1) Takbiratul-Ihram (Allahu Akbar) to start, (2) Recitation of Surah al-Fatiha in first two rak\'ahs, (3) A complete surah after al-Fatiha, (4) Dhikr in ruku (Subhana Rabbiyal-Azimi wa bihamdih or 3x Subhanallah), (5) Dhikr in sujud (Subhana Rabbiyal-A\'la wa bihamdih or 3x Subhanallah), (6) Tashahhud, (7) Salam to end prayer. Correct pronunciation is important — one should learn from a qualified teacher.',source:'Minhaj al-Salihin',tags:['dhikr','prayer','obligatory','recitation','ruku','sujud']},
{id:'sl104',cat:'salat',sub:'Dhikr',marja:'khamenei',q:'What are the obligatory dhikr in prayer?',a:'The minimum obligatory adhkar: Takbirat al-Ihram, Fatiha + one surah (rak\'ahs 1-2), dhikr in ruku and sujud (minimum: Subhanallah 3 times each), tasbihat al-arba\'ah in 3rd and 4th rak\'ahs (or Fatiha), tashahhud, and final salam. Learning proper Arabic pronunciation is important. If one cannot learn, they should recite to the best of their ability while continuing to learn.',source:'Ajwibat al-Istifta\'at',tags:['dhikr','prayer','obligatory','recitation','ruku','sujud']},
{id:'sl105',cat:'salat',sub:'Dhikr',marja:'makarem',q:'What are the obligatory dhikr in prayer?',a:'Essential prayer components with their minimum dhikr: Standing (qiyam) with Fatiha and surah, bowing (ruku) with glorification, two prostrations (sujud) with glorification, sitting for tashahhud bearing witness, and ending with salam. Each dhikr should be recited with presence of heart (hudur al-qalb) — the spirit of prayer is mindful connection with Allah, not mere verbal recitation.',source:'Tawdih al-Masail',tags:['dhikr','prayer','obligatory','recitation','ruku','sujud']},
{id:'sl106',cat:'salat',sub:'Salat al-Ayat',marja:'sistani',q:'When is Salat al-Ayat (prayer for signs) obligatory?',a:'Salat al-Ayat becomes obligatory upon: (1) Solar eclipse, (2) Lunar eclipse, (3) Earthquake, (4) Any natural phenomenon that causes widespread fear (thunderstorms, hurricanes, unusual darkness). It is 2 rak\'ahs with 5 ruku in each rak\'ah (total 10 ruku). It must be performed on the day of the event if possible. For eclipses, it should be performed during the eclipse. If one misses it, qada is obligatory.',source:'Minhaj al-Salihin',tags:['ayat','eclipse','earthquake','obligatory','signs']},
{id:'sl107',cat:'salat',sub:'Salat al-Ayat',marja:'khamenei',q:'When is Salat al-Ayat (prayer for signs) obligatory?',a:'Salat al-Ayat is obligatory for eclipses, earthquakes, and other fear-inducing natural events. The prayer has a unique format: 2 rak\'ahs, each with 5 standing-bowing cycles. It reminds humans of their dependence on Allah and the signs of His power in nature. In modern times, eclipses are predicted — one should prepare to perform this prayer when an eclipse occurs in their region.',source:'Ajwibat al-Istifta\'at',tags:['ayat','eclipse','earthquake','obligatory','signs']},
{id:'sl108',cat:'salat',sub:'Night Prayer',marja:'sistani',q:'What is Salat al-Layl (night prayer)?',a:'Salat al-Layl (Tahajjud) is highly recommended — 11 rak\'ahs performed in the last third of the night: 8 rak\'ahs in 2-rak\'ah sets (nafilah), 2 rak\'ahs of Salat al-Shaf\'a, and 1 rak\'ah of Salat al-Witr. It is one of the most emphasized mustahab prayers. The Quran says: "And during part of the night, pray — it may be that your Lord will raise you to a praiseworthy station" (17:79). It was never missed by the Prophet (s) and Imams (as).',source:'Minhaj al-Salihin',tags:['night prayer','tahajjud','layl','witr','recommended']},
{id:'sl109',cat:'salat',sub:'Night Prayer',marja:'khamenei',q:'What is Salat al-Layl (night prayer)?',a:'Salat al-Layl is the most virtuous of nafilah prayers. Time: from midnight until Fajr (the last third of the night is best). If one cannot wake, it can be prayed before sleeping with the intention of performing it early. The 11 rak\'ahs include the beautiful Qunut of Witr prayer where one can pray for 40 believers by name. Regular night prayer transforms one\'s spiritual state and is the hallmark of the devoted believer.',source:'Ajwibat al-Istifta\'at',tags:['night prayer','tahajjud','layl','witr','recommended']},
{id:'sl110',cat:'salat',sub:'Night Prayer',marja:'makarem',q:'What is Salat al-Layl (night prayer)?',a:'The night prayer is described by Imam Sadiq (as) as "the honor of the believer." Its benefits include: answered duas (especially in Qunut of Witr), spiritual elevation, light in the face, good character, removal of anxiety, and closeness to Allah. Even performing the Shaf\'a and Witr alone (3 rak\'ahs) when unable to do all 11 is highly rewarded. Consistency in night prayer, even if short, is better than occasional long prayers.',source:'Tawdih al-Masail',tags:['night prayer','tahajjud','layl','witr','recommended']},
,
{id:'sl111',cat:'salat',sub:'Obligatory Prayers',marja:'sistani',q:'What are the times for the five daily prayers?',a:'(1) Fajr: from true dawn (subh sadiq) until sunrise, (2) Zuhr: from solar noon (zawal) — when the sun begins to decline, (3) Asr: after zuhr prayer until sunset — though the preferred time is before the shadow of an object equals its length, (4) Maghrib: after sunset when the eastern redness disappears, (5) Isha: after maghrib prayer until midnight (religious midnight = halfway between sunset and fajr). Zuhr and Asr can be combined, as can Maghrib and Isha.',source:'Minhaj al-Salihin',tags:['times','five','fajr','sunset']},
{id:'sl112',cat:'salat',sub:'Obligatory Prayers',marja:'khamenei',q:'What are the times for the five daily prayers?',a:'Prayer times: Fajr begins at true dawn and ends at sunrise. Zuhr enters at solar noon. In Shia practice, zuhr and asr can be combined — meaning asr can follow immediately after zuhr. Similarly, isha can follow immediately after maghrib. The specific (fadilah) times are preferred but the shared times are valid. This combining is a significant practical facilitation in Shia fiqh.',source:'Ajwibat al-Istifta\'at',tags:['times','combining','fajr','zuhr']},
{id:'sl113',cat:'salat',sub:'Qada',marja:'sistani',q:'What is the ruling on missed prayers (qada)?',a:'Making up missed prayers is obligatory for every prayer intentionally missed or missed due to sleep or forgetfulness. If too many are accumulated, one should make them up gradually but consistently. Prayers missed during menstruation or unconsciousness do not require qada. The eldest son must make up his deceased father\'s missed prayers or arrange for someone to do so. There is no time limit for making up qada prayers.',source:'Minhaj al-Salihin',tags:['qada','missed','obligatory','eldest son']},
{id:'sl114',cat:'salat',sub:'Friday Prayer',marja:'sistani',q:'What is the ruling on Friday prayer (Salat al-Jumu\'ah)?',a:'During the occultation of the Imam (a), Friday prayer is wajib takhyiri — meaning one can choose between performing Friday prayer or the regular zuhr prayer. If Friday prayer is established with all conditions (qualified imam leading, two khutbahs, minimum congregation), attending it is sufficient and zuhr is not needed. Some scholars consider it obligatory (wajib ayni) during occultation while others consider it recommended.',source:'Minhaj al-Salihin',tags:['friday','jumuah','takhyiri','occultation']},
{id:'sl115',cat:'salat',sub:'Traveler\'s Prayer',marja:'sistani',q:'How does prayer change for a traveler?',a:'A traveler meeting specific conditions must shorten four-rakat prayers (zuhr, asr, isha) to two rakats. Conditions: traveling at least 8 farsakh (approximately 44 km) one way, the journey is not their profession, they have a specific destination, and they don\'t intend to stay 10+ days at the destination. If staying 10+ days, they pray complete. The traveler also does not fast during Ramadan but must make up the fasts later.',source:'Minhaj al-Salihin',tags:['traveler','shortened','distance','conditions']},
{id:'sl116',cat:'salat',sub:'Prostration',marja:'khamenei',q:'What surface is valid for prostration (sujud)?',a:'In Shia practice, prostration must be on earth or what grows from it (excluding edibles and wearables). Valid surfaces: soil, stone, clay tablet (turbah), wood, paper, leaves, straw. Invalid: carpet, cloth, synthetic materials, food items, metals. The turbah of Karbala is recommended due to its connection to Imam Husayn (a). The forehead must touch the surface — placing it on the back of one\'s hand on carpet is not valid.',source:'Ajwibat al-Istifta\'at',tags:['prostration','sujud','turbah','earth']},
{id:'sl117',cat:'salat',sub:'Congregational',marja:'makarem',q:'What are the benefits and rules of congregational prayer?',a:'Congregational prayer (salat al-jamaah) carries immense reward — exponentially more than individual prayer. The imam must be just (adil), of legitimate birth, and able to recite correctly. The follower must hear or see the imam or the row ahead. Women can pray in congregation led by a woman. Late joiners (masbuq) follow specific rules for catching up. Congregational prayer strengthens community bonds.',source:'Tawdih al-Masail',tags:['congregational','jamaah','reward','imam']},
{id:'sl118',cat:'salat',sub:'Night Prayer',marja:'sistani',q:'What is Salat al-Layl (night prayer)?',a:'Salat al-Layl is 11 rakats prayed in the last third of the night: eight rakats in four sets of two (nafilah), two rakats of Shaf, and one rakat of Witr. It is among the most rewarded voluntary prayers. The Quran references night prayer as a practice of the righteous. It develops closeness to Allah, provides spiritual strength, and is particularly powerful for dua. Even praying a portion is beneficial.',source:'Minhaj al-Salihin',tags:['night prayer','salat al-layl','witr','voluntary']}
,
{id:'sl119',cat:'salat',sub:'Dhikr',marja:'sistani',q:'What are the obligatory dhikrs in prayer?',a:'Obligatory recitations: (1) Surah al-Fatiha in the first two rakats, (2) A complete surah after Fatiha in those rakats, (3) Tasbihat al-Arba (Subhan Allahi wal hamdu lillahi wa la ilaha illallahu wallahu akbar) three times or once in the third and fourth rakats (or Fatiha alone), (4) Dhikr in ruku (Subhana Rabbi al-Azim wa bihamdih), (5) Dhikr in sujud (Subhana Rabbi al-Ala wa bihamdih), (6) Tashahhud in second and last rakat, (7) Salam.',source:'Minhaj al-Salihin',tags:['dhikr','obligatory','fatiha','tasbihat']},
{id:'sl120',cat:'salat',sub:'Mistakes',marja:'khamenei',q:'What should one do if they make a mistake in prayer?',a:'If you add an extra ruku or sujud intentionally, the prayer is void. If unintentional: an extra sujud does not void the prayer; an extra ruku does. If you forget a ruku or both sujuds, the prayer is void. If you forget one sujud, continue and perform it after salam as qada. For doubts about rakat count: in a 4-rakat prayer, if you doubt between 3 and 4, assume 4 and pray 1 rakat of ihtiyat standing.',source:'Ajwibat al-Istifta\'at',tags:['mistakes','extra','doubt','ihtiyat']},
{id:'sl121',cat:'salat',sub:'Mustahab',marja:'makarem',q:'What are some recommended practices during prayer?',a:'Recommended acts include: qunut (supplication in second rakat before ruku), prolonged dhikr in ruku and sujud beyond the minimum, specific surahs for specific prayers (Ya-Sin for Fajr, etc.), praying in the early part of the time window, wearing pleasant fragrance, brushing teeth before prayer, focusing the gaze on the place of sujud, and maintaining khushu (humble presence of heart) throughout.',source:'Tawdih al-Masail',tags:['recommended','qunut','khushu','fragrance']},
{id:'sl122',cat:'salat',sub:'Special Prayers',marja:'sistani',q:'What is Salat al-Ayat (prayer for signs)?',a:'Salat al-Ayat is obligatory during: solar eclipse, lunar eclipse, earthquakes, and any frightening natural phenomenon (severe storms, abnormal darkness). It consists of 2 rakats, each with 5 rukus and 2 sujuds. In each rakat: recite Fatiha, then a portion of any surah, do ruku, rise, continue the surah, ruku again — repeating 5 times, then 2 sujuds. If performed after the event has passed, it is qada.',source:'Minhaj al-Salihin',tags:['ayat','eclipse','earthquake','obligatory']}
,
{id:'sl124',cat:'salat',sub:'Times',marja:'sistani',q:'What are the exact prayer times in Islam?',a:'Fajr: from true dawn (appearance of horizontal whiteness in the east) until sunrise. Dhuhr: from when the sun passes its zenith until the time needed for Asr prayer before sunset. Asr: after Dhuhr prayer until sunset. Maghrib: after sunset (when redness in the eastern sky disappears according to precaution) until midnight. Isha: after Maghrib prayer until midnight. Each prayer has a preferred time (fadilah) within its window.',source:'Minhaj al-Salihin',tags:['times','fajr','dhuhr','maghrib']},
{id:'sl125',cat:'salat',sub:'Times',marja:'khamenei',q:'What are the exact prayer times in Islam?',a:'Prayer times: Fajr begins at true dawn and ends at sunrise. Dhuhr and Asr share the time from solar noon to sunset — Dhuhr is prayed first. Maghrib and Isha share from sunset to midnight — Maghrib first. The specific time for each is determined by astronomical calculation. Using reliable prayer time apps and calendars is fine. If a prayer is missed accidentally, it must be made up (qada) as soon as remembered.',source:'Ajwibat al-Istifta\'at',tags:['times','calculation','apps','qada']},
{id:'sl126',cat:'salat',sub:'Qada',marja:'sistani',q:'What are the rules for making up missed prayers?',a:'Missed obligatory prayers must be made up (qada). There is no specific order required between qada prayers, though maintaining the original order (Dhuhr before Asr) within the same day is recommended. Qada can be performed at any time — day or night. A son should make up his deceased father\'s missed prayers (obligatory on the eldest son). If the number of missed prayers is unknown, one should continue making up until reasonably certain all are covered.',source:'Minhaj al-Salihin',tags:['qada','missed','order','eldest son']},
{id:'sl127',cat:'salat',sub:'Congregational',marja:'sistani',q:'What are the rewards and rules of congregational prayer?',a:'Congregational prayer is highly recommended. Its reward increases exponentially with each additional person. Rules: the imam must be just (adil), male (for men\'s congregation), of legitimate birth, and must recite correctly. Followers do not recite al-Fatiha and surah in the first two rakaat (the imam recites for them). Physical connection between rows is required. Women can form their own congregation with a female imam.',source:'Minhaj al-Salihin',tags:['congregational','reward','imam','conditions']},
{id:'sl128',cat:'salat',sub:'Traveler',marja:'sistani',q:'What are the rules for a traveler\'s prayer?',a:'A traveler who meets the conditions (approximately 44 km journey, not frequent traveler, not traveling for sin) must shorten 4-rakaat prayers to 2 rakaat. Fajr (2 rakaat) and Maghrib (3 rakaat) remain unchanged. Conditions: intention to travel the required distance, departure from city limits, and not intending to stay 10+ days at the destination. If staying 10+ days, prayers revert to full. A frequent traveler prays full.',source:'Minhaj al-Salihin',tags:['traveler','shortened','distance','conditions']},
{id:'sl129',cat:'salat',sub:'Prostration',marja:'khamenei',q:'What surfaces are valid for prostration (sujud)?',a:'Prostration must be on earth or what grows from earth (not eaten or worn). Valid: earth, stone, sand, clay tablet (turbah), wood, leaves, grass, paper (made from wood). Invalid: fabric, plastic, metal, carpet, food items, and anything synthetic. The most recommended is the earth of Karbala. The forehead must rest firmly on the valid surface. Using a turbah is the most practical solution.',source:'Ajwibat al-Istifta\'at',tags:['sujud','prostration','turbah','earth']},
{id:'sl130',cat:'salat',sub:'Friday Prayer',marja:'sistani',q:'What is the ruling on Friday prayer in Shia Islam?',a:'Friday prayer (Salat al-Jumuah) is wajib takhyiri during the occultation — meaning one can choose between Friday prayer (2 rakaat with 2 khutbahs) or regular Dhuhr prayer (4 rakaat). If one prays Friday prayer, Dhuhr is not needed. Conditions: led by a qualified imam, minimum attendance, the two sermons must be delivered. During the Imam\'s presence, it would be individually obligatory.',source:'Minhaj al-Salihin',tags:['friday','jumuah','optional','dhuhr']}
,
{id:'sl131',cat:'salat',sub:'Doubts',marja:'sistani',q:'How does one handle doubts during prayer?',a:'Rules for doubts: (1) In a 2-rakaat prayer, any doubt about the number invalidates it, (2) In a 3-rakaat prayer, doubt about whether you are in the 2nd or 3rd rakaat — build on the higher and add one standing rakaat after, (3) In a 4-rakaat prayer, multiple doubt scenarios with specific remedies (ihtiyat prayers). The general principle: build on the higher number and compensate with precautionary prayer. Excessive doubt (kathir al-shakk) should be ignored.',source:'Minhaj al-Salihin',tags:['doubts','prayer','ihtiyat','ignore']},
{id:'sl132',cat:'salat',sub:'Sujud Sahw',marja:'khamenei',q:'When are the prostrations of forgetfulness required?',a:'Sujud sahw (two extra prostrations) are required when: (1) One accidentally talks during prayer, (2) One accidentally recites salam at the wrong place, (3) One forgets one sajdah but remembers after standing, (4) One forgets tashahhud. The method: after the final salam of prayer, immediately perform two sajdahs with the dhikr, then tashahhud and salam. These prostrations rectify minor errors that don\'t invalidate the prayer.',source:'Ajwibat al-Istifta\'at',tags:['sujud sahw','forgetfulness','errors','correction']},
{id:'sl133',cat:'salat',sub:'Qiblah',marja:'sistani',q:'How does one determine the qiblah direction?',a:'Methods to find qiblah: (1) Reliable compass apps/devices, (2) Knowledge of local mosque orientation, (3) Asking knowledgeable local Muslims, (4) Using the position of the sun/stars, (5) Any reasonable method of estimation. If the qiblah direction cannot be determined after effort, pray in the direction you believe most likely. If completely uncertain with no way to determine it, some scholars say pray in four directions. Modern technology makes this rarely necessary.',source:'Minhaj al-Salihin',tags:['qiblah','direction','compass','estimation']}
,
{id:'sl200',cat:'salat',sub:'Mistakes',marja:'sistani',q:'What happens if one realizes they prayed in the wrong direction?',a:'If after prayer you discover the qiblah was significantly wrong: if the deviation was 90 degrees or more, the prayer must be repeated. If the deviation was less than 90 degrees, the prayer is valid. If you realize during prayer that you are facing the wrong direction, correct yourself immediately and continue from where you are — you do not restart. The effort to find qiblah before prayer satisfies the obligation.',source:'Minhaj al-Salihin',tags:['qiblah','wrong','direction','repeat']},
{id:'sl201',cat:'salat',sub:'Mistakes',marja:'khamenei',q:'What happens if one realizes they prayed in the wrong direction?',a:'If the deviation from qiblah was slight (within the acceptable range), the prayer is valid. If significantly off (more than 45 degrees left or right), repeat the prayer if still within its time. If time has passed, no qada is needed as you made genuine effort. During prayer, if you realize you are off, turn toward the correct qiblah and continue — do not restart unless the deviation was toward your back.',source:'Ajwibat al-Istifta\'at',tags:['qiblah','deviation','correction','valid']},
{id:'sl202',cat:'salat',sub:'Recitation',marja:'sistani',q:'What surahs can be recited after al-Fatiha in prayer?',a:'After al-Fatiha in the first two rakaat, one complete surah must be recited. Any surah from the Quran is permissible except: Surah al-Fil and Surah Quraysh (which some scholars consider one surah — recite both if choosing either), and the four obligatory sajdah surahs (Alaq, Najm, Sajdah, Fussilat) should not be recited in obligatory prayer as the sajdah would disrupt the prayer format.',source:'Minhaj al-Salihin',tags:['surah','recitation','prayer','exceptions']},
{id:'sl203',cat:'salat',sub:'Recitation',marja:'khamenei',q:'What surahs can be recited after al-Fatiha in prayer?',a:'Any complete surah may be recited after al-Fatiha except: the four surahs containing obligatory sajdah (since performing sajdah during prayer would disrupt the sequence). Surah al-Ikhlas and Surah al-Qadr are among the most commonly recited due to their brevity and reward. In the third and fourth rakaat, the choice is between al-Fatiha, tasbihat al-arba\'a (three times), or a combination.',source:'Ajwibat al-Istifta\'at',tags:['surah','recitation','sajdah','tasbihat']},
{id:'sl204',cat:'salat',sub:'Latecomer',marja:'sistani',q:'How does a latecomer join congregational prayer?',a:'If you arrive late: join the imam wherever he is. If you catch the imam in ruku, that counts as catching that rakaat. If you join during the third or fourth rakaat, it becomes your first rakaat — you recite al-Fatiha and surah silently while the imam recites tasbihat. After the imam\'s salam, stand up and complete your remaining rakaat independently. Joining congregational prayer at any point is better than praying alone.',source:'Minhaj al-Salihin',tags:['latecomer','congregational','joining','ruku']},
{id:'sl205',cat:'salat',sub:'Latecomer',marja:'khamenei',q:'How does a latecomer join congregational prayer?',a:'Join the congregation at whatever point you arrive. If you catch the imam in ruku, you have caught that rakaat. The rakaat you join counts as your first. If the imam is in his third rakaat, it becomes your first — recite silently. After the imam finishes, complete your remaining rakaat. Important: do not rush — it is better to join calmly in the next rakaat than to rush and join improperly.',source:'Ajwibat al-Istifta\'at',tags:['latecomer','join','calm','congregation']},
{id:'sl206',cat:'salat',sub:'Witr',marja:'sistani',q:'What is Salat al-Layl (night prayer) and its importance?',a:'Salat al-Layl is 11 rakaat performed in the last third of the night before fajr: 8 rakaat (in pairs) of nafilah, then 2 rakaat of shaf, then 1 rakaat of witr. The witr prayer includes qunut with personal dua and seeking forgiveness for 40 believers. This prayer is among the most recommended. The Quran says: \'And in a part of the night, pray — it may be that your Lord will raise you to a praised station\' (17:79).',source:'Minhaj al-Salihin',tags:['night prayer','layl','witr','recommended']},
{id:'sl207',cat:'salat',sub:'Witr',marja:'khamenei',q:'What is Salat al-Layl (night prayer) and its importance?',a:'The night prayer consists of 11 rakaat: 4 pairs (8 rakaat) of nafilah, 1 pair (shaf), and 1 single rakaat (witr). It is performed after midnight, ideally in the last third of the night. Its reward is immense — it brings spiritual illumination, answers to prayers, and divine closeness. The qunut in witr is a powerful time for personal supplication. Even performing a shortened version (shaf and witr only) is valuable.',source:'Ajwibat al-Istifta\'at',tags:['night prayer','importance','qunut','spiritual']},
{id:'sl208',cat:'salat',sub:'Prostration',marja:'sistani',q:'What dhikr is recited in ruku and sujud?',a:'In ruku, the minimum is: \'Subhana Rabbiyal Azimi wa bihamdih\' (Glory to my Lord the Great, and praise be to Him) once, though three times is recommended. In sujud: \'Subhana Rabbiyal A\'la wa bihamdih\' (Glory to my Lord the Most High, and praise be to Him) once, three times recommended. Alternatively, \'Subhanallah\' three times suffices for both. Rushing through these dhikr invalidates the prayer if the minimum is not completed.',source:'Minhaj al-Salihin',tags:['dhikr','ruku','sujud','minimum']},
{id:'sl209',cat:'salat',sub:'Prostration',marja:'khamenei',q:'What dhikr is recited in ruku and sujud?',a:'The obligatory dhikr in ruku is at minimum \'Subhana Rabbiyal Azimi wa bihamdih\' once or \'Subhanallah\' three times. In sujud: \'Subhana Rabbiyal A\'la wa bihamdih\' once or \'Subhanallah\' three times. The body must be still and settled before beginning the dhikr. Moving during the dhikr invalidates it. Additional dhikr and dua in sujud are highly recommended — sujud is the closest a servant is to Allah.',source:'Ajwibat al-Istifta\'at',tags:['dhikr','ruku','sujud','stillness']}
,
{id:'sl210',cat:'salat',sub:'Tashahhud',marja:'sistani',q:'What is recited in the tashahhud and salam of prayer?',a:'In tashahhud (sitting after 2nd and last rakaat): \'Ashhadu an la ilaha illallah, wahdahu la sharika lah, wa ashhadu anna Muhammadan abduhu wa rasuluh. Allahumma salli ala Muhammad wa ali Muhammad.\' In the final salam: \'Assalamu alayka ayyuhan nabiyyu wa rahmatullahi wa barakatuh. Assalamu alayna wa ala ibadillahis salihin. Assalamu alaykum wa rahmatullahi wa barakatuh.\' The salam ends the prayer.',source:'Minhaj al-Salihin',tags:['tashahhud','salam','recitation','formula']},
{id:'sl211',cat:'salat',sub:'Tashahhud',marja:'khamenei',q:'What is recited in the tashahhud and salam of prayer?',a:'The tashahhud includes the two testimonies of faith and salawat upon the Prophet and his family. It is recited while sitting after every second rakaat and in the final rakaat. The salam addresses the Prophet (s), then all righteous servants, then all present. Saying \'Assalamu alaykum wa rahmatullahi wa barakatuh\' is the concluding salam. One may suffice with just the third salam, though reciting all three is recommended.',source:'Ajwibat al-Istifta\'at',tags:['tashahhud','salam','sitting','formula']},
{id:'sl212',cat:'salat',sub:'Qunut',marja:'sistani',q:'What is qunut in prayer and when is it performed?',a:'Qunut is raising the hands in supplication during prayer, performed in the second rakaat before going into ruku. It is mustahab (recommended) in all obligatory and recommended prayers. Any dua can be recited — there is no specific required text. Commonly recited: \'Rabbana atina fid-dunya hasanah wa fil akhirati hasanah wa qina adhab an-nar.\' Omitting qunut does not invalidate the prayer but loses the recommended reward.',source:'Minhaj al-Salihin',tags:['qunut','dua','second rakaat','recommended']},
{id:'sl213',cat:'salat',sub:'Qunut',marja:'khamenei',q:'What is qunut in prayer and when is it performed?',a:'Qunut is a recommended supplication in the second rakaat of every prayer, performed by raising both hands before ruku. Any dua in any language is acceptable, though Arabic is preferred. Special qunut is performed in Salat al-Witr (night prayer) where one seeks forgiveness for 40 believers by name. In Salat al-Eid, qunut is performed in each rakaat. Qunut is a powerful moment of personal connection with Allah during prayer.',source:'Ajwibat al-Istifta\'at',tags:['qunut','recommended','witr','eid']}
,
{id:'sl300',cat:'salat',sub:'Eid Prayer',marja:'sistani',q:'How is Eid prayer performed?',a:'Eid prayer is 2 rakaat. First rakaat: after al-Fatiha and surah, perform 5 takbirats with qunut (dua) after each. Then ruku and sujud. Second rakaat: after al-Fatiha and surah, perform 4 takbirats with qunut after each. Then ruku, sujud, tashahhud, and salam. During occultation, Eid prayer is mustahab (recommended) and can be prayed individually or in congregation. The specific duas for each qunut are recommended but any dua suffices.',source:'Minhaj al-Salihin',tags:['eid','prayer','takbirat','qunut']},
{id:'sl301',cat:'salat',sub:'Eid Prayer',marja:'khamenei',q:'How is Eid prayer performed?',a:'Eid prayer consists of 2 rakaat with additional takbirats and qunuts. In the first rakaat: 5 extra takbirats, each followed by qunut. In the second: 4 extra takbirats with qunut. Eid al-Fitr prayer is offered on 1st Shawwal after Ramadan, Eid al-Adha on 10th Dhul Hijjah. While recommended during occultation, it becomes obligatory during the Imam\'s presence. Performing it in congregation is strongly recommended.',source:'Ajwibat al-Istifta\'at',tags:['eid','prayer','congregation','recommended']},
{id:'sl302',cat:'salat',sub:'Ayat Prayer',marja:'sistani',q:'When is Salat al-Ayat required?',a:'Salat al-Ayat (prayer of signs) becomes obligatory upon: solar eclipse, lunar eclipse, earthquakes, and any terrifying natural phenomenon (major thunderstorms, hurricanes, unusual darkness). It is 2 rakaat with 5 ruku in each rakaat — after al-Fatiha, recite part of a surah, go to ruku, stand, continue the surah, ruku, and so on for 5 ruku per rakaat. It can be prayed individually or in congregation.',source:'Minhaj al-Salihin',tags:['ayat','eclipse','earthquake','obligatory']},
{id:'sl303',cat:'salat',sub:'Ayat Prayer',marja:'khamenei',q:'When is Salat al-Ayat required?',a:'Salat al-Ayat becomes wajib for: solar and lunar eclipses (even partial), earthquakes (even minor ones felt), and terrifying natural phenomena. The prayer has 2 rakaat, each containing 5 ruku. It must be performed on the same day/night of the event. For eclipses, it should be completed during the eclipse if possible. If missed, it must be made up. This prayer reminds believers of Allah\'s power over creation.',source:'Ajwibat al-Istifta\'at',tags:['ayat','eclipse','earthquake','wajib']},
{id:'sl304',cat:'salat',sub:'Nawafil',marja:'sistani',q:'What are the daily recommended prayers (nawafil)?',a:'Daily nawafil total 34 rakaat: Fajr nafilah (2 before fajr prayer), Dhuhr nafilah (8 before dhuhr), Asr nafilah (8 before asr), Maghrib nafilah (4 after maghrib), Isha nafilah (2 sitting = 1 rakaat standing, after isha), and Salat al-Layl (11 rakaat in last third of night). On Fridays, dhuhr and asr nawafil increase to 10 each. These prayers bring immense spiritual reward and closeness to Allah.',source:'Minhaj al-Salihin',tags:['nawafil','recommended','daily','34']},
{id:'sl305',cat:'salat',sub:'Nawafil',marja:'khamenei',q:'What are the daily recommended prayers (nawafil)?',a:'The daily recommended prayers supplement obligatory worship: 2 before fajr, 8 before dhuhr, 8 before asr, 4 after maghrib, 2 after isha (prayed sitting, counts as 1 standing), and the night prayer (11 rakaat). The Prophet (s) said these nawafil compensate for deficiencies in obligatory prayers. Even performing a portion of them is rewarding. The night prayer in particular carries special merit.',source:'Ajwibat al-Istifta\'at',tags:['nawafil','supplement','night prayer','merit']},
{id:'sl306',cat:'salat',sub:'Concentration',marja:'sistani',q:'How can one improve concentration (khushu) in prayer?',a:'Khushu (humility and focus) is the soul of prayer. Tips: (1) Understand the meaning of what you recite, (2) Pray in a quiet, clean place, (3) Remember you are standing before Allah, (4) Avoid praying when distracted (hungry, needing bathroom, sleepy), (5) Look at the place of sujud, (6) Pray at the earliest time, (7) Remove distractions from your surroundings, (8) Reflect on death before prayer. The Prophet (s) said: pray as if it is your last prayer.',source:'Minhaj al-Salihin',tags:['khushu','concentration','focus','tips']},
{id:'sl307',cat:'salat',sub:'Concentration',marja:'khamenei',q:'How can one improve concentration (khushu) in prayer?',a:'Achieving khushu requires effort: understand the Arabic recitations, contemplate each movement\'s meaning (sujud = ultimate submission), choose a focused time, avoid praying hurriedly, maintain consciousness of Allah\'s presence, remove physical distractions, and gradually build focus over time. A prayer with khushu — even short — is better than a long prayer without it. The key is quality of connection, not just quantity of words.',source:'Ajwibat al-Istifta\'at',tags:['khushu','quality','contemplation','presence']},
{id:'sl308',cat:'salat',sub:'Clothes',marja:'sistani',q:'Can one pray with najis clothing if no clean clothes are available?',a:'If only najis clothing is available and one cannot obtain clean clothing: pray in the najis clothing rather than miss the prayer entirely. If even that is not possible (completely undressed), pray seated with gestures for ruku and sujud to maintain covering. The principle: prayer should never be abandoned — if ideal conditions cannot be met, pray in the best way possible. Missing prayer intentionally is a major sin.',source:'Minhaj al-Salihin',tags:['najis','clothing','necessity','prayer']},
{id:'sl309',cat:'salat',sub:'Clothes',marja:'khamenei',q:'Can one pray with najis clothing if no clean clothes are available?',a:'If clean clothing is genuinely unavailable and the prayer time will pass: pray in the najis clothes. It is better to pray with najasah than to miss prayer entirely. Some scholars say pray once with najis clothes and once naked (combining both opinions as precaution). If time only allows one prayer, pray in the najis clothing. Allah judges by circumstances and intention — He does not burden a soul beyond its capacity.',source:'Ajwibat al-Istifta\'at',tags:['najis','unavailable','necessity','intention']}
];